---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import { Card, Alert, Badge } from "webcoreui/astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Maindeck Analysis - Replenish Guide"
    description="Deep dive into the core cards and strategy of the Replenish deck. Understand the purpose of every enchantment, cantrip, and interaction spell in the maindeck and sideboard."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Maindeck Analysis"
        description="The post-ban Replenish maindeck operates as a calculated combo-control architecture. Every slot across the 60 cards is strictly allocated to balance early deck velocity, resilient mana development, proactive defense against fast hostile metagames. Below is an analytical breakdown of the mandatory staples and flex configurations that define contemporary winning lists."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/attunement.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Attunement"
        imageCaption="Attunement by Randy Gallegos"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card title="The Core" titleTag="h2">
        <CardGrid
            width="180px"
            cards={[
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/sorceries/replenish.jpg",
                    alt: "Replenish",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/opalescence.jpg",
                    alt: "Opalescence",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/parallax_wave.webp",
                    alt: "Parallax Wave",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/attunement.jpg",
                    alt: "Attunement",
                },
            ]}
        />

        <div class="core-cards-list">
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Replenish</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    The non-negotiable cornerstone of the archetype. Delivering
                    massive, asymmetric tempo and permanent swings, running four
                    copies is strictly essential regardless of your
                    configuration.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Opalescence</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    Transforms all non-Aura enchantments into creatures with
                    power and toughness equal to their mana value. Note that
                    Opalescence does not animate itself—a second copy on the
                    battlefield is required to animate the first into a 4/4.
                    Under a single Opalescence, your primary threats become
                    massive combat clocks: Decree of Silence (8/8), Parallax
                    Wave (4/4), Attunement (3/3), and Seal of Cleansing (2/2).
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Parallax Wave</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    Serves a dual role as early tactical defense and lethal
                    end-game engine. Defensively, it controls hostile creature
                    boards; paired with Opalescence, it initiates a permanent,
                    one-sided creature exile loop while presenting a 4/4 body.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="core-card-header">
                    <h3 class="core-card-title">Attunement</h3>
                    <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                        >4 Main · 100% Inclusion</Badge
                    >
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    The foundational selection engine that digs three cards
                    deep, curates the graveyard for Replenish, and attacks as a
                    3/3 under Opalescence. Avoid blind activations. Treat
                    Attunement as a precise tactical tool rather than casual
                    draw; before activating, explicitly identify the cards you
                    intend to discard. If your current hand structure already
                    dictates your upcoming sequence and lacks redundant discard
                    fodder, preserve your hand and hold the activation.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Lock Piece" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Decree of Silence"
            badge="2–3 Main · 98.3% Inclusion"
            image="enchantments/decree_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Replacing Parallax Tide as the premier lock piece, Decree has
                proven exceptional across multiple tactical axes. While
                assembling the complete Opal-Wave-Decree combo guarantees
                immediate victory, simply discarding Decree to Attunement or
                Frantic Search and returning it via Replenish forces opponents
                to expend massive resources fighting through three consecutive
                countered spells.
            </p>
            <p>
                In control matchups, cycling Decree for six mana serves as an
                uncounterable cantrip that dismantles key countermagic or
                game-winning threats while maintaining card flow. Opponents
                frequently pass it off an Intuition pile assuming an eight-mana
                enchantment is inert, only to lose immense tempo to its cycling
                trigger. Furthermore, under Opalescence, it hits the battlefield
                as a commanding 8/8 finisher.
            </p>
            <p>
                For an analytical breakdown of when to field two versus three
                copies across various metagames, reference our architectural
                comparison on the <a href="/deck" class="guide-link"
                    >Lists page</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Disruption" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            badge="0–4 Main · 10.6% MD / 92.2% SB"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                While traditional configurations reserve creatures for
                post-sideboard transformation to render opposing Game 1 spot
                removal dead, running Meddling Mage in the maindeck provides an
                immediate, highly disruptive shield against fast combo and
                permission-dense control decks.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Operating Meddling Mage effectively requires precise metagame
                anticipation and understanding of enemy bottleneck spells. For
                exhaustive matchup-by-match naming conventions and
                transformational sideboarding principles, consult our dedicated <a
                    href="/sideboard"
                    class="guide-link">Sideboard Analysis</a
                > and <a href="/matchups" class="guide-link">Matchup Matrix</a>.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Abeyance"
            badge="2–3 Main · 72.8% MD / 42.2% SB"
            image="instants/abeyance.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A crucial staple in modern competitive lists, Abeyance functions
                as both an unassailable protective shield for your combo turns
                and a disruptive tempo instrument against hostile decks. Because
                it restricts opponents from casting instants, sorceries, or
                activating permanent abilities until end of turn, resolving it
                immediately prior to casting <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > guarantees uninterrupted reanimation while silencing artifact interaction
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Furthermore, since Abeyance replaces itself by drawing a card
                upon resolution, it can be aggressively cast during an
                opponent's upkeep to stall development turns, shut off <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a
                > activations, or incapacitate threats with activated abilities.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Card Selection" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Frantic Search"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/frantic_search.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Provides essential deck velocity and enables explosive
                development turns when paired with sol lands or painlands.
                However, because it incurs intrinsic card disadvantage, it is
                the most frequently trimmed tool post-sideboard against grinding
                control strategies where resource conservation is critical.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Intuition"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/intuition.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The archetype's most versatile selection engine and
                skill-testing tool. The standard combo pile is Opalescence,
                Parallax Wave, and Decree of Silence—guaranteeing that whichever
                piece the opponent selects, the other two fuel a subsequent
                Replenish. Yet piles must adapt dynamically to the board state:
                against Landstill, searching for three lands; against fast
                aggro, tutoring for three copies of Swords to Plowshares ensures
                survival. Trimming copies against reactive blue decks is common
                practice to preserve overall threat density. For an exhaustive
                masterclass on generating tactical piles and navigating tricky
                board states, consult our dedicated <a
                    href="/intuition"
                    class="guide-link">Intuition Cheatsheet</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Why Not Careful Study?"
            badge="Omitted from Maindeck"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                While <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Careful Study">Careful Study</a
                > appears tempting as a one-mana graveyard sculpting engine, competitive
                theory rules it out as a structural sub-optimum. Unlike <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > (which remains a recurring enchantment engine) or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > (which fuels burst untaps on sol lands and painless velocity), Careful
                Study incurs strict card disadvantage (a -1 net resource loss immediately
                upon resolution). In our combo-control architecture, bleeding card
                advantage simply to dump enchantments exhausts our critical mass of
                permission and defense before reaching the lethal four-mana Replenish
                threshold.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Interaction & Permission" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Swords to Plowshares"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="instants/swords_to_plowshares.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The gold standard of one-mana spot removal. Three copies in the
                maindeck keep the strategy efficient and streamlined, while
                bringing in the fourth post-sideboard solidifies your defenses
                against creature-heavy builds.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Cleansing"
            badge="2–3 Main · 97.8% Inclusion"
            image="enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Provides proactive interaction against problematic enchantments
                and mana artifacts. Because it can be sacrificed for instant
                tactical value and reanimated via Replenish, it functions as a
                recurring control engine. Easily sideboarded out against
                opponents lacking targets.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Mana Leak"
            badge="2–3 Main · 35.6% Inclusion"
            image="instants/mana_leak.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Running Mana Leak in the maindeck is a proven metagame
                adjustment, fielded by over 35% of post-ban tournament pilots.
                In aggressive environments featuring fast combo or reactive
                mirrors like Esper Psychatog and tempo Replenish builds, cheap
                two-mana countermagic provides critical early-turn protection.
                While conditional surcharges degrade during long grinding games
                against <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Standstill">Landstill</a
                >, holding open two mana forces opponents into sub-optimal
                sequences during your setup turns.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Disruption & Mana Denial" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tsabo’s Web"
            badge="1–3 Main · 24.4% MD / 77.8% SB"
            image="artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                A premier proactive answer to opponent utility lands like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dust Bowl"
                    >Dust Bowl</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Wasteland"
                    >Wasteland</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mishra's Factory">Mishra's Factory</a
                >. Repositioning this artifact out of general removal and into
                proactive mana denial highlights its architectural role: casting
                it early completely neutralizes enemy taxing lands and attacking
                creature-lands before they can constrain our setup or pressure
                our combo timelines, while instantly cantripping to preserve
                card velocity.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Grinding & Card Advantage Engines" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Deep Analysis"
            badge="0–1 Flex · 3.3% Inclusion"
            image="sorceries/deep_analysis.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A potent utility piece that transforms an early <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > into a sustainable card-advantage engine during slower matchups.
                Against permission-heavy control, tutoring directly for combo pieces
                risks exposing vital threats to graveyard exiles; targeting Deep Analysis
                instead guarantees persistent draw power from both hand and graveyard.
                Including a single maindeck copy is an effective tool for out-grinding
                traditional draw-go structures.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Fact or Fiction"
            badge="0–2 Flex · 2.8% Inclusion"
            image="instants/fact_or_fiction.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An established option for reactive, instant-speed variations
                that hold open countermagic suites like Mana Leak and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Counterspell">Counterspell</a
                >. While a four-mana investment is demanding, digging five cards
                deep offers efficient end-of-turn selection, allowing you to
                quickly deposit discarded enchantments into the graveyard or
                sculpt defensive answers without relinquishing tempo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Mana Base" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Mana Base Theory</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>25 vs. 26 Lands:</strong> Contemporary tournament setups split
            cleanly between 25 and 26 total lands, with both quantities representing
            battle-tested, valid configurations. Pilots operating on 25 lands typically
            trim down to three copies of Skycloud Expanse to preserve maximum basic
            land density against Wasteland and Price of Progress. Conversely, players
            opting for 26 lands almost universally dedicate the additional slot to
            completing the full four-of suite of Skycloud Expanse, maximizing dual-color
            filtering and guaranteeing smooth, uninterrupted mana sequencing into
            four-mana turns.
        </p>
        <div>
            <div class="core-card-header">
                <h3 class="core-card-title">Ten Basics</h3>
                <Badge theme="secondary" small={true}
                    >10 Main (5 Island · 5 Plains)</Badge
                >
            </div>
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Running five basic Islands and five basic Plains insulates
                the strategy against aggressive non-basic punishment like
                Wasteland and Price of Progress. Securing your first three
                land drops as basics is a crucial stabilizing line against
                burn and mana-denial builds. Providing ~16 sources of each
                color to cast double-pipped four-drops on schedule.
            </p>
        </div>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Ancient Tomb"
            badge="4 Main · 100% Inclusion"
            image="lands/ancient_tomb.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                As an essential "sol land" producing two colorless mana on turn
                one, Ancient Tomb serves as the primary velocity accelerator of
                the archetype. It enables explosive setups by fueling turn-two <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Intuition">Intuition</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >, while deploying critical turn-three combo pieces like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                > ahead of schedule without sacrificing future land drops.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                Furthermore, untapping Ancient Tomb via Frantic Search actually
                nets an extra colorless mana upon resolution, generating immense
                burst turns even under mana chokeholds. Because presence of
                Ancient Tomb alters early spell math, it frequently transforms
                borderline opening hands into automatic keeps; consult our <a
                    href="/mulligan#the-ancient-tomb-acceleration-factor"
                    class="guide-link">Mulligan Guide</a
                > and <a href="/mechanics" class="guide-link"
                    >Mechanics Masterclass</a
                > for deeper technical breakdowns.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Skycloud Expanse"
            badge="3–4 Main · 99.4% Inclusion"
            image="lands/skycloud_expanse.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The core fixing filter that converts colorless mana from sol
                lands and basic lands into double-pipped blue or white symbols.
                Running three copies in 25-land builds intentionally prioritizes
                resilience against non-basic hate, whereas running the full four
                copies in 26-land structures ensures absolute color stability
                for heavy spell turns.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Why Not City of Traitors?"
            badge="Omitted from Maindeck"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Without <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                > to rapidly truncate games, post-ban Replenish relies heavily on
                mid-to-late game tactical stamina.
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="City of Traitors"
                    >City of Traitors</a
                > imposes severe sequencing constraints that actively degrade your
                board presence in prolonged attrition battles.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <Alert theme="warning">
                <strong>Turn-One Sequencing</strong><br />When evaluating your
                opening plays, leading with a dual land is generally your best
                move to maximize color flexibility for whatever you draw on turn
                two. However, if you suspect your opponent is running Wasteland,
                leading with a basic land is the safer path to avoid losing
                tempo.
            </Alert>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card>
        <p
            style="margin-bottom: 0; text-align: center; color: var(--w-color-primary-30);"
        >
            Now that you know the 60, explore the full Sideboard Analysis to
            complete your 75.
        </p>
    </Card>
</Layout>

<style>
    .core-cards-list {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 1.5rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
    }

    .core-card-header {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.75rem;
        margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
    }

    .core-card-title {
        margin: 0;
        color: var(--w-color-primary-10);
    }
</style>
